    "story_text": "Sakura answered the entry with no wasted motion and met the greeting head-on. \"This is the Hanezawa residence,\" she said, and the words landed as a statement for the record rather than an opening for pleasantries. She offered nothing further—not ease, not warmth, not the kind of reflexive welcome that would have to be withdrawn later. The caller had used the household's name correctly, which counted for something, but it was a starting point, not a bond. She left the exchange standing at the threshold, unopened.\n\nFormality was not the same as rudeness, and Sakura had no use for either pretense. \"This household is expecting a visitor from Tokyo,\" she said. \"You have arrived at the right house and named it correctly. That narrows the possibilities. It does not close them.\" The pause she left was its own demand. \"State your name, and we will know whether the expected visitor and the caller are the same person. I will not guess further than the evidence carries.\"\n\nShe let the silence do the work she had assigned it. No elaboration, no softened restatement, no easy exit from the question she had placed at the center of the exchange. The threshold remained what it was: a point of verification, and the household's boundary did not move on its own. If the name matched the expectation, the arrival would proceed along its proper course. If it did not, that would be addressed with equal firmness. The next word belonged to the caller. Sakura held the doorway of the Hanezawa house the way she held everything—exactly, and without yielding ground she had not yet been given.",
